## Why This Is Asked

Real-time network paths require precise tail latency insight without burdening performance. eBPF offers low-overhead, per-event visibility ideal for diagnosing bursts in UDP data paths.

## Key Concepts

- eBPF tracing (bpftrace/bpfcc) for live latency data
- Tracepoints vs kprobes for kernel events
- Per-CPU histograms and BPF maps for aggregation
- Data-path isolation with tc and minimal instrumentation
- Safe rollback/guardrails if overhead grows

## Code Example

```javascript
function percentile(arr, p){
  if (!arr.length) return 0;
  const sorted = [...arr].sort((a,b)=>a-b);
  const idx = Math.ceil((sorted.length-1)*p/100);
  return sorted[idx];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this plan if TLS/DTLS terminates before the RX path?
- What thresholds and sampling rates would you choose for a fleet of hosts, and how would you automate rollback across them?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests practical problem solving for shared Linux runtimes. It focuses on beginner-friendly CPU isolation using cgroup v2 primitives rather than app changes.

## Key Concepts
- cgroup v2 CPU controller (cpu.max, cpu.weight)
- systemd slices or cpuset for per-tenant isolation
- basic benchmarking of latency under bursts

## Code Example
```javascript
// Reads current cpu.max for a given cgroup path
const fs = require('fs');
try {
  const quota = fs.readFileSync('/sys/fs/cgroup/cpu.slice/cpu.max','utf8').trim();
  console.log('cpu.max:', quota);
} catch (e) { console.error(e); }
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this plan to handle bursty tenants with varying baselines?
- What risks exist with aggressive quotas and how would you detect them?
